+# CSV import
+
+## What and why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import creates new contacts, updates existing
+ones (matched by email), and returns a report of what happened per row so Sales can fix
+the spreadsheet and re-import. The file has the same columns as our CSV export
+(`name,email,phone,tags`, tags joined with `;` in one cell).
+
+## Interface (fixed by the request — verbatim)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+`ImportFileError` is also re-exported from `contactbook`.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number** — 1-based physical line on which a record starts; the header is row 1 and
+  every line counts, blank lines included (so it matches the spreadsheet row).
+- **Contact row** — a data row (after the header) that is not blank. A **blank row** has no
+  cells or only cells that are empty / whitespace (e.g. Excel's `,,,`).
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Decoding and file-level checks (`ImportFileError`, repository untouched)
+
+1. `bytes` are decoded as UTF-8; a leading UTF-8 BOM is accepted. `str` input that starts
+   with U+FEFF is accepted the same way.
+2. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError` whose message tells the user to
+   re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8". There is no fallback encoding.
+3. An empty file (no header row: zero length, or only blank lines) raises `ImportFileError`.
+4. The first line is the header. Header cells are matched
+   case-insensitively with surrounding whitespace ignored; column order is free.
+5. The name comes from a `name` column, or else from the pair `first_name` + `last_name`
+   (joined with one space). A header with neither `name` nor both of `first_name` and
+   `last_name` raises `ImportFileError`. A header without `email` raises `ImportFileError`.
+6. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+7. A header naming the same recognised column twice raises `ImportFileError`. *(decision)*
+8. More than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError` and nothing is imported. Exactly
+   1000 is accepted. Blank rows do not count.
+9. Malformed CSV quoting (an unterminated quoted cell, text after a closing quote) raises
+   `ImportFileError` rather than silently swallowing the following rows into one cell. A cell
+   longer than the standard `csv` field limit (131072 characters) is also a file error. *(decision)*
+10. Only `,` is a delimiter. A `;`- or tab-delimited file raises `ImportFileError` whose message
+    says columns must be delimited by commas (also when its quoting breaks the CSV parse).
+
+### Header warnings (never errors)
+
+11. Each unrecognised header column produces one warning `RowIssue(row=1, column=None, ...)`
+    naming it; its cells are ignored.
+12. If `name` is present, `first_name`/`last_name` are unrecognised (criterion 11). *(decision)*
+13. A header-only file returns an empty report.
+
+### Rows
+
+14. Blank rows are skipped silently (no issue, not counted toward the limit).
+15. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as empty.
+16. A row with more cells than the header is an error (`column=None`) unless every extra
+    cell is blank, in which case the extras are ignored. Its fields are still checked and
+    reported (criteria 18, 19). *(decision)*
+17. Values are validated with the existing validators (`validation.py` / `Contact.create`):
+    - name: from `name`, or `first_name + " " + last_name`; invalid → error, `column="name"`.
+    - email: required and valid, else error `column="email"`.
+    - phone: blank → "no value"; otherwise must be valid, else error `column="phone"`.
+    - tags: cell split on `;`, empty pieces ignored; any invalid tag → error `column="tags"`.
+18. Every invalid field of a row is reported (one `RowIssue` per field), and the row is
+    skipped entirely. *(decision)*
+19. An email appearing again in a later row (compared after normalisation) is an error on the
+    later row, `column="email"`, message naming the earlier row; the later row is not applied.
+    The first row with that email "claims" it even if that row is itself rejected for another
+    field. *(decision)*
+20. A rejected row changes nothing in the repository, not even partly.
+
+### Applying rows (partial import: good rows are saved even if others fail)
+
+21. A valid row whose email is not in the repository creates the contact; its normalised
+    email is appended to `created`.
+22. A valid row whose email is in the repository (case-insensitive) updates it:
+    - name is replaced by the file's name;
+    - phone is replaced only if the cell has a value — a blank cell or missing `phone`
+      column never clears an existing phone;
+    - the file's tags are added to the existing tags; an import never removes tags.
+    The normalised email is appended to `updated`.
+23. If the merged contact equals the stored one, nothing is written and the email is in
+    neither `created` nor `updated`.
+24. `created`, `updated`, `errors` and `warnings` are in file order.
+25. Exported CSV (`export_csv`) imported into an empty repository recreates equal contacts.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8; delimiter sniffing; files without a header.
+- Deleting contacts or removing tags/phones via import.
+- A dry-run mode, transactions across the whole file, or persistence beyond the given repository.
+- CLI / HTTP wiring (other packages).
+- Column aliases beyond `first_name`/`last_name`.
+
+## Scope limits
+
+Touches `errors.py`, `__init__.py`, new `importer.py`, new `tests/test_importer.py`.
+No new dependencies (standard library only).
